KNOWNBUG: N5008 [temp.class.spec.match] + [temp.variadic].  A recursive class
template PARTIAL SPECIALIZATION over a NON-type parameter pack (`sum_t<H, T...>`
with `v = H + sum_t<T...>::v`) fails to fold for EXACTLY TWO elements
(`sum_t<2,3>::v` "could not fully type-check ... unsupported construct"), while
0/1/3/4-element sums fold correctly and the TYPE-pack analogue is fine.  The
PRIMARY-template non-type pack forwarding collapse this once shared a root with
is now fixed (cpp11_nontype_pack_forward_collapse and
cpp11_nontype_pack_sizeof_expr_forwarded are CORE).

PRECISE ROOT (localized 2026-07-10): while instantiating `sum_t<2,3>` its member
initializer references `sum_t<3>` (the trailing 1-element pack `T={3}` forwarded
to a 0-element trailing pack).  Elaborating `sum_t<3>` performs partial-
specialization matching, which re-type-checks the specialization PATTERN
`<H, T...>` via typecheck_template_args under an SFINAE context.  For the empty
deduced trailing pack (`T={}`) that call throws (`throw 0`), so the candidate is
skipped and `sum_t<3>` falls back to the INCOMPLETE PRIMARY (a forward
declaration with no members).  At top level a later member-access completion
recovers `sum_t<3>`, but inside `sum_t<2,3>`'s member initializer (an SFINAE +
suppress_elaborate context) the failure is final, so `sum_t<3>::v` stays an
unresolved cpp_name and `H + sum_t<3>::v` cannot be type-checked.  Confirmed:
pre-instantiating any `cc<single>` first makes `cc<2,3>` succeed.  Fix requires
the partial-spec pattern re-type-check to tolerate an empty deduced trailing pack
(so `sum_t<3>` matches its specialization on first elaboration).  Flip to CORE
once a recursive non-type-pack partial-specialization fold is correct at two
elements.
